> > > > }
> > > > model{
> > > >   ...
> > > >  for(i in 1:Nmiss_phy){
> > > >        phyto_miss[i] ~ poisson(phyto_miss_lambda[i]);
> > > >      }
> > > > }"
>
> Is there any recommended work around in this case?  I saw the f <https://groups.google.com/forum/?fromgroups=#!forum/stan-users>ollowing: https://groups.google.com/forum/?fromgroups=#!searchin/stan-users/integer$20parameter/stan-users/MJf_SwNmGFo/AvVS1qPVGP0J, but couldn't readily see how it might be applied in my case.

It seems that my integer hack doesn't really work in practice. But poisson() can take a real (nonnegative) parameter for its expectation, so you could use that.

Ben

On Tuesday, October 2, 2012 7:49:20 AM UTC-4, atzap wrote:
> Really??  I thought the expected value of a Poisson has to be discrete (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poisson_distribution)...

The expected value (lambda) can be any positive number.

> The STAN reference manual (v1.0.1) also says on pg 122 that the expectation "n" has to be an integer.  It threw up an error that suggested this when I tried switching my missing parameter to a "real".

But this is a problem with the current implementation of Stan. The poisson() function in Stan expects the "data" to be nonnegative integers, which is a problem when the "data" are missing and the missing value is a parameter.

There are lots of hits on Google about continuous generalizations of the Poisson distribution. This guy

http://cmscience.blogspot.com/2008/04/mt-6-poisson-and-gamma-distribution.html

has a simple one, which just replaces the factorial with a gamma function. The comments ask him to show that the continuous distribution integrates to something finite, which he doesn't do, but I'll presume that is always so, in which case you could take the log and do something like this in Stan

parameters {
  real<lower=0> phyto_miss[Nmiss_phy];
}
model {
  for(i in 1:Nmiss_phy) {
    lp__ <- lp__ + phyto_miss[i] * log(phyto_miss_lambda[i]) -
                   phyto_miss_lambda[i] - lgamma(phyto_miss[i] + 1.0);
  }
}

Or, as the link above points out, you could use a gamma distribution for the missing values.

Ben

Hi all

Thanks for your help with my last problem.  I've managed to get past the compiling stage but have predictably run into a new problem.  Unfortunately, I can't seem to figure this one out and reckon other folk might run into this as well...

I'm trying to standardized some parameters to a mean of zero and sd of one for use later in my model.  I've tried doing this as follows (this is a simplified version of my model):

data{
 int<lower=0> N;
 int<lower=0> y[N];
 int<lower=0> y2[N];
 int<lower=0> y3[N];
 int<lower=0> n[N];
 real CA[N];
}
parameters{
 real lambda[3];
}
transformed parameters{
real<lower = 0, upper = 1> pBound[N];
real<lower = 0, upper = 1> pBound2[N];
real<lower = 0, upper = 1> pBound3[N];
real pBound2_centered[N];
real pBound3_centered[N];
  for(i in 1:N){
   pBound[i] <- fmax(0, fmin(1, inv_logit(lambda[1]) ) );
   pBound2[i] <- fmax(0, fmin(1, inv_logit(lambda[2] * CA[i]) ) );
   pBound3[i] <- fmax(0, fmin(1, inv_logit(lambda[3] * CA[i]) ) );
  }
  for(i in 1:N){
    pBound2_centered[i] <- (pBound2[i] - mean(pBound2))/sd(pBound2);
    pBound3_centered[i] <- (pBound3[i] - mean(pBound3))/sd(pBound3);
  }
}
model{
  for (i in 1:N){
   y[i] ~ binomial(n[i], pBound[i]);
   y2[i] ~ binomial(n[i], pBound2[i]);
   y3[i] ~ binomial(n[i], pBound3[i]);
  }
  lambda ~ normal(0, 1E3);
}

The model compiles but then fails to sample, calling the following error:
> Error in sampler$call_sampler(args_list[[i]]) : std::bad_alloc

I can replace:
> pBound2_centered[i] <- (pBound2[i] - mean(pBound2))/sd(pBound2)

With:
> pBound2_centered[i] <- (pBound2[i] - mean(pBound2))

And the model gets sampled.  But, it doesn't work if I try and replace this with:
> pBound2_centered[i] <- (pBound2[i] - sd(pBound2))

So, I have two questions.
1.  What is the error specifically telling me in this case?

2.  What is the proper way to standardize a parameter in STAN?  I note that I tried the "stacks" example from BUGS (see http://code.google.com/p/stan/source/browse/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/stacks/stacks_a_normal.stan), where standardization is done in the transformed data block, and it runs fine.  So clearly, I'm missing something with respect to trying to standardize parameters.

Many thanks for your help and patience,
Andrew

Sorry, I should have added that I not only replace "pBound2_centered" but also "pBound3_centered" in each example.

